{{also|BANANA|banána|banāna|banānā|banană|Appendix:Variations of "banana"}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|wo|banaana}}, via {{der|en|es|-}} or {{der|en|pt|-}}, of unknown origin, but potentially from {{der|en|ar|بَنَان||fingertip}}<ref>Oxford Dictionaries citing John Ayto’s The Diner’s Dictionary: Word origins of food and drink [https://web.archive.org/web/20170710073834/http://blog.oxforddictionaries.com/2014/08/origin-banana/]</ref>.

In reference to well-assimilated East Asians, derived from the fruit's foreign origins, and the slur that they are "[[yellow]] (Asian) on the outside, [[white]] (influenced by Western culture) on the inside".
{{root|en|ar|ب و ن}}<!--possibly-->
[[File:Bananavarieties.jpg|thumb|Four different types of bananas. The larger yellow bananas on the far right are commercially dominant Cavendish bananas.]]

